  "$schema": "subscription-review-report/1",
  "verdict": "PASS_WITH_NOTES",
  "confidence": "high",
  "source_coverage": {
    "TARGET.md": "entire supplied text: damped-norms section (definitions, L:damped-homogeneous-norms and proof) and complete spatial-occupation module (L:spatial-physical-pair through P:occupation-physical-class and zero-covariance paragraph)",
    "CONTEXT.md": "entire supplied text: H:homog and zero covariance, R_alpha definition, unitary/spectral convention, existing positive-control scores, and existing multiplier consumers",
    "DIFF.md": "entire supplied diff: hunks for notes/integrated_spatial_occupation.tex (new L:spatial-fourier-bound and modified P:spatial-smooth-ratio) and notes/notes_stratonovich_ham_second_moment.tex (modified L:damped-homogeneous-norms)",
    "note": "The prompt did not provide numbered line ranges for TARGET.md/CONTEXT.md; the full files were read as supplied."
  },
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "claim": "L:spatial-fourier-bound handles H:homog with alpha<2, b>0, zero covariance, and singular Gamma/mu cases.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "The b>0 argument is valid: a C_c^infty unit L2 function with zero full gradient is a constant and must vanish. The proof works exclusively with the measure Gamma and its spectral measure mu, requiring no pointwise density; c_Q is C_c^infty so the Gamma pairing is finite even for singular Gamma, and |F0 Q|^2 is Schwartz so the mu pairing is finite even for the white-noise spectral measure. The zero case is handled: Gamma=0 forces mu=0 by tempered-distribution Fourier uniqueness, so R_alpha=0 and both sides of the bound vanish. The threshold alpha0+alpha<2 together with alpha0>0 gives alpha<2, so the resolvent exponent is admissible."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The proof uses the unnormalized Fourier convention consistently, obtains the autocorrelation Schwartz identity, has finite integrals, and performs no illicit Fubini over two infinite oscillatory measures.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "F0 is explicitly defined without the unitary c_d and is stated as c_d^{-1}widehat Q in the masked-section convention. The spectral identity E:masked-spectral-convention is applied as a distributional pairing; for c_Q the inner e^{i xi y} integral is an ordinary absolutely convergent integral because c_Q is compactly supported. The equality A_sp(g)=int |F0 Q|^2 mu(dxi) is distributional pairing, not an interchange of two sigma-finite oscillatory measures, and the text explicitly disclaims such an interchange."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The Fourier/gradient estimates, the majorant 8b/(4b+|xi|^2), the resolvent identity, and the constant 2^(1+alpha)R_alpha are correct.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "|F0 Q| <= ||Q||_1 = 1 and |xi||F0 Q| <= ||grad Q||_1 <= 2||g||_2||grad g||_2 = 2sqrt b are valid. The two-region argument gives the stated majorant. The resolvent computation int mu(dxi)/(q^2+|xi|^2) = q^{alpha-2}R_alpha follows from mu(B_r)=r^alpha mu(B_1) and int_0^infty u^{alpha-1}/(1+u^2)du = pi/(2 sin(pi alpha/2)); with q=2sqrt b this yields 8b R_Gamma(2sqrt b)=2^{1+alpha}R_alpha b^{alpha/2}."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "R0 finiteness is now deterministic; strict positivity is local to Gamma nonzero; there is no cycle with T:full-upper or S1Bdd; and the score argument keeps its finite-real S1^pos premise.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "L:spatial-fourier-bound gives R(g) <= 2^{1+alpha}R_alpha uniformly for smooth unit profiles, so R0 is finite without appealing to finiteness of S1^pos. Positivity of A_sp(g) for a nonzero smooth nonnegative bump uses the autocorrelation positivity near zero and the homogeneity-induced positivity of every origin-centered Gamma ball, valid for nonzero Gamma. The score inequality in P:spatial-smooth-ratio still uses L:spatial-static-controls, whose proof legitimately uses the fixed finite-real S1^pos from P:variational-positive-scores; there is no circular dependency on T:full-upper through R0 anymore."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "N_{1,m,S}, N_{2,m,S}^2, arbitrary measurable m in [0,1], measurable nested positive Laplace domains, and their finiteness are well-defined.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "m mu is a positive finite-on-compacts measure because m is measurable in [0,1] and mu is a positive Radon measure. S subset S' subset (0,infty) keeps the lambda integration away from any endpoint pathology. Since all integrands are nonnegative, N_{1,m,S} <= N_{1,1,S'} <= N_1(1) and N_{2,m,S}^2 <= N_{2,1,S'}^2 <= N_2(1)^2, so the preliminary finiteness of N_1 and N_2 transfers. The notation N_{2,m,S}^2 is explicitly defined as the whole expression, not as the square of a separately defined quantity."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "N2 has a nonnegative time quadratic form at each (lambda,xi), the integral exchanges are justified, monotonicity follows, and no pointwise sign claim is made for products of wave Fourier kernels.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "B_theta(lambda,xi) is the time integral against the positive-definite kernel e^{-lambda|s-t|}, so B_theta >= 0. The proof uses the already established absolute-integrability majorant, 0<=m<=1, and 0<=K_S<=|r|^{-alpha0} to justify exchanging the time, lambda, and xi integrals. The comparison then uses nonnegativity after time integration, and the text explicitly states this is not a pointwise claim about widehat G_s overline{widehat G_t}."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "Exact scaling identities remain for the original homogeneous covariance; arbitrary m is not asserted to define a new H:homog model; Gaussian smoothing and the separate N0 mass argument are covered.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "The N0, N1, and N2 scaling under the full original covariance are preserved in the lemma. The text explicitly states that an arbitrary m need not preserve H:homog and that scaling is asserted only for the original covariance. Gaussian spatial and Green smoothing are identified as multipliers in the same m-class, and Green spatial smoothing is separately handled for N0 because G_s(R^d)=s is preserved by normalized spatial convolution."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "claim": "Full/tail comparisons, temporal tails, and the included existing consumers remain valid.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "Choosing S=S'=(0,infty) gives the full bounds and S=S'=(M,infty) gives the tail bounds. N1^(M) tends to zero as the tail of a finite nonnegative integral. For N2^(M), 0<=K_M<=|r|^{-alpha0} and K_M->0 for r<>0 with the absolute majorant integrable, so dominated convergence applies on the null-diagonal complement. The comparison statement is exactly the multiplier-removal mechanism used by the existing smoothing consumers."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "claim": "The diff introduces no mathematical weakening in P:spatial-smooth-ratio, and the downstream four-linear extension and energy identification in TARGET.md remain valid.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "The change replaces the S1^pos-dependent proof of R0 finiteness by a deterministic bound and otherwise preserves the score-inequality argument. Downstream, P:spatial-four-linear only needs a finite positive R0 (now supplied deterministically) and the continuity/extension argument is unchanged; P:spatial-energy-map uses R_sp=R0 and the same energy identity. No needed conclusion is lost."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "claim": "Global scope: cross-label consistency, inherited assumptions, substantive unproved steps, and remaining blockers.",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "Labels such as L:spatial-fourier-bound, L:spatial-static-controls, P:spatial-smooth-ratio, L:damped-homogeneous-norms and E:intro-spectral-mass are consistent. The only substantive cross-reference is to the resolvent calculation inside the proof of L:damped-homogeneous-norms, which is valid. No blocker or unresolved mathematical gap was found in the scoped material; only minor exposition issues were noted."
    }
  ],
  "findings": {
    "fatal": [],
    "major": [],
    "minor": [
      {
        "id": "M1",
        "claim": "The damped-norms proof reduces radial spectral integrals using only the ball-mass law mu(B_r)=r^alpha V.",
        "reason": "This is correct by layer-cake/Stieltjes integration for the monotone radial integrands used, but the proof compresses the justification and does not state the layer-cake step explicitly."
      },
      {
        "id": "M2",
        "claim": "Positive definiteness of e^{-lambda|s-t|} is invoked to get B_theta >= 0.",
        "reason": "This is a standard fact (Cauchy characteristic or Fourier transform of a positive measure), but its proof is not included; one line would make the argument self-contained."
      },
      {
        "id": "M3",
        "claim": "The notation N_{2,m,S}(theta)^2 denotes a defined expression rather than the square of a scalar N_{2,m,S}(theta).",
        "reason": "The prose defines the symbol explicitly, but the suffix '^2' could invite the wrong reading."
      },
      {
        "id": "M4",
        "claim": "L:spatial-fourier-bound cites a resolvent computation from inside the proof of L:damped-homogeneous-norms rather than a numbered standalone equation.",
        "reason": "The computation is valid and visible in the supplied TARGET.md, but promoting it to a labeled equation would improve cross-referencing."
      }
    ]
  },
  "required_repairs": [],
  "limitations": "Review is scoped to the supplied changed prose and its stated interfaces; it is not a whole-manuscript acceptance, novelty assessment, or image review. Only TARGET.md, CONTEXT.md, DIFF.md, and the environment instructions were read. No attempt was made to consult unseen surrounding lemmas beyond the supplied context. Line-numbered source files were not available, so coverage is recorded by file and section."
}

